{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 48 kg, height 163 cm (1.63 m). BMI = 48 kg ÷ (1.63 m)² ≈ 48 ÷ 2.6569 ≈ 18.1 kg/m². This is <18.5, so the patient is underweight. 2. For underweight patients, the adjusted body weight = actual body weight = 48 kg. 3. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for men: CrCl (mL/min) = [(140 – age) × weight (kg)] ÷ (72 × serum creatinine (mg/dL)). 4. Plug in the values: age = 87 years, weight = 48 kg, serum creatinine = 1.4 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 87) × 48 = 53 × 48 = 2544. Denominator = 72 × 1.4 = 100.8. 5. CrCl = 2544 ÷ 100.8 ≈ 25.2 mL/min. 6. Rounded to the nearest whole number, the creatinine clearance is about 25 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈ 25 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Identify the admission values: weight = 67.2 kg, height = 173 cm (1.73 m), serum creatinine = 1.4 mg/dL, age = 83 years, male. 2. Calculate BMI: BMI = 67.2 kg / (1.73 m)² ≈ 22.5 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 3. Compute Ideal Body Weight (IBW) for a male: IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height = 173 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 68.1 inches → excess = 8.1 inches. IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 × 8.1 ≈ 68.7 kg. 4. Since BMI is normal, set adjusted body weight to the minimum of IBW and actual weight: min(68.7 kg, 67.2 kg) = 67.2 kg. 5. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation (male, no 0.85 factor): CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight] / (72 × serum creatinine) = [(140 – 83) × 67.2] / (72 × 1.4). Numerator = 57 × 67.2 = 3830.4. Denominator = 72 × 1.4 = 100.8. CrCl = 3830.4 / 100.8 = 38 mL/min.",
  "answer": "38 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Determine if the patient is overweight/obese using BMI. BMI = 28.1, which is >25, so the patient is overweight. Therefore we must use the adjusted body weight (AdjBW). 2. Calculate Ideal Body Weight (IBW) for a female: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (inches over 5 ft). Height = 1.60 m = 62.99 in ≈ 63 in. Inches over 5 ft = 63 in – 60 in = 3 in. IBW = 45.5 + 2.3×3 = 52.4 kg. 3. Compute AdjBW: AdjBW = IBW + 0.4 × (Actual Weight – IBW) = 52.4 + 0.4 × (72 kg – 52.4 kg) = 52.4 + 0.4 × 19.6 = 52.4 + 7.84 = 60.24 kg. 4. Convert serum creatinine from μmol/L to mg/dL (Cockcroft‑Gault uses mg/dL). 1 mg/dL = 88.4 μmol/L, so 85 μmol/L ÷ 88.4 = 0.962 mg/dL. 5. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for females: CrCl = ((140 – age) × weight × 0.85) / (SCr × 72). Plug in age = 62 yr, weight = AdjBW = 60.24 kg, SCr = 0.962 mg/dL. Numerator = (140‑62) × 60.24 × 0.85 = 78 × 60.24 × 0.85 = 3,993.9. Denominator = 0.962 × 72 = 69.264. CrCl = 3,993.9 ÷ 69.264 ≈ 57.7 mL/min. 6. Round to a practical clinical value: ≈ 58 mL/min.",
  "answer": "58 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 105 kg / (1.60 m)^2 = 41 kg/m² → obese. 2. Use adjusted body weight (ABW) because BMI >30. 3. Ideal body weight (IBW) for women = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height = 5 ft 3 in (63 in). IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg×3 = 52.4 kg. 4. ABW = IBW + 0.4 × (actual weight – IBW) = 52.4 kg + 0.4 × (105 kg – 52.4 kg) = 52.4 kg + 0.4 × 52.6 kg = 52.4 kg + 21.04 kg = 73.44 kg. 5. Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = ((140 – age) × weight × 0.85) / (72 × serum creatinine). 6. Plug in values: age = 42, weight = 73.44 kg, serum creatinine = 0.8 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 42) × 73.44 × 0.85 = 98 × 73.44 × 0.85 ≈ 6,117.55. Denominator = 72 × 0.8 = 57.6. 7. CrCl = 6,117.55 / 57.6 ≈ 106.2 mL/min. 8. Round to nearest whole number: ≈106 mL/min.",
  "answer": "106 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 58 kg / (1.56 m)^2 = 58 / 2.4336 ≈ 23.8 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 2. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight (ABW) to the lower of ideal body weight (IBW) and actual weight. 3. Compute IBW for a woman: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (height‑5 ft). Height 156 cm = 5 ft 1.4 in (≈1.42 in over 5 ft). IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × 1.42 ≈ 45.5 kg + 3.3 kg = 48.8 kg. 4. Minimum of IBW (48.8 kg) and actual weight (58 kg) is 48.8 kg, so ABW = 48.8 kg. 5. Apply Cockcroft‑Gault for women: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW × 0.85] / (72 × serum creatinine). Age = 45 y, serum creatinine = 0.7 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 45) × 48.8 × 0.85 = 95 × 48.8 × 0.85 ≈ 3,940.6. Denominator = 72 × 0.7 = 50.4. 6. CrCl = 3,940.6 / 50.4 ≈ 78.2 mL/min. 7. Round to a reasonable clinical value: ≈ 78 mL/min.",
  "answer": "78 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Extract the required data at admission: age = 71 years, sex = male, serum creatinine = 1.42 mg/dL, height = 155 cm, weight = 59 kg. 2. Calculate BMI = weight (kg) / (height (m))² = 59 kg / (1.55 m)² ≈ 24.5 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5–24.9). 3. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight (ABW) to the minimum of ideal body weight (IBW) and actual weight. 4. Compute IBW for a man: IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height in inches = 155 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 61.0 in. IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (61.0 – 60) ≈ 52.35 kg. 5. Minimum of IBW (52.35 kg) and actual weight (59 kg) = 52.35 kg, so ABW = 52.35 kg. 6. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for males: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW] ÷ (72 × serum creatinine). Numerator = (140 – 71) × 52.35 = 69 × 52.35 ≈ 3612.15. Denominator = 72 × 1.42 = 102.24. 7. CrCl = 3612.15 ÷ 102.24 ≈ 35.3 mL/min. 8. Round to a reasonable clinical precision: about 35 mL/min.",
  "answer": "35.3 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Determine the patient's BMI: weight 53 kg / (1.53 m)^2 = 22.6 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 2. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight (ABW) to the minimum of the ideal body weight (IBW) and the actual weight. 3. Calculate IBW for a woman: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height = 153 cm = 60.24 in, so IBW ≈ 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × 0.24 ≈ 46.0 kg. 4. Minimum of IBW (46.0 kg) and actual weight (53 kg) = 46.0 kg. Thus ABW = 46.0 kg. 5. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW × 0.85] / (SCr × 72). 6. Plug in values: age = 82 yr, ABW = 46.0 kg, serum creatinine (SCr) = 3.26 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 82) × 46.0 × 0.85 = 58 × 46.0 × 0.85 = 2,267.8. Denominator = 3.26 × 72 = 234.72. 7. CrCl = 2,267.8 / 234.72 ≈ 9.66 mL/min. 8. Round to a reasonable clinical precision: ≈ 9.7 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈ 9.7 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 60 kg / (1.60 m)^2 = 23.4 kg/m² → normal range (18.5‑24.9). 2. Because BMI is normal, set adjusted body weight (ABW) to the lower of ideal body weight (IBW) and actual weight. 3. Compute IBW for a female using the Devine formula: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height 160 cm ≈ 63 inches, so IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × 3 = 52.4 kg. 4. Minimum of IBW (52.4 kg) and actual weight (60 kg) is 52.4 kg → ABW = 52.4 kg. 5. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for females: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW × 0.85] / (72 × serum creatinine). Age = 47 yr, serum creatinine = 0.90 mg/dL. 6. Numerator: (140 – 47) = 93; 93 × 52.4 kg = 4 873.2; 4 873.2 × 0.85 = 4 142.22. 7. Denominator: 72 × 0.90 = 64.8. 8. CrCl = 4 142.22 / 64.8 ≈ 63.9 mL/min, which rounds to about 64 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈64 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Identify the admission data (prior to any treatment): age = 59 years, sex = female, height = 154.2 cm, weight = 44.0 kg, serum creatinine = 4.2 mg/dL. 2. Calculate BMI: height in meters = 1.542 m; BMI = 44 kg / (1.542 m)² ≈ 44 / 2.378 ≈ 18.5 kg/m². This is at the lower end of the normal range (≥18.5), so the patient is not overweight/obese. 3. Compute ideal body weight (IBW) for a woman: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (inches over 5 ft). Height = 154.2 cm = 60.63 in; inches over 5 ft = 0.63 in. IBW ≈ 45.5 kg + 2.3 × 0.63 ≈ 45.5 kg + 1.45 kg ≈ 46.9 kg. 4. Since BMI is normal, set adjusted body weight to the minimum of IBW and actual weight: min(46.9 kg, 44.0 kg) = 44.0 kg. 5. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for females: CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight × 0.85] / (72 × serum Cr). Plugging in the numbers: (140 – 59) = 81; numerator = 81 × 44.0 kg × 0.85 = 3029.4. Denominator = 72 × 4.2 = 302.4. CrCl = 3029.4 / 302.4 ≈ 10.0 mL/min. 6. Round to a reasonable clinical precision: approximately 10 mL/min.",
  "answer": "10 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Calculate BMI: weight 59 kg / (height 1.68 m)^2 = 59 / 2.8224 ≈ 20.9 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 2. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight to the minimum of the ideal body weight (IBW) and the actual weight. 3. Compute IBW for a male: IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height 168 cm = 66.14 in, so excess = 6.14 in. IBW = 50 + 2.3 × 6.14 ≈ 64.1 kg. 4. Minimum of IBW (64.1 kg) and actual weight (59 kg) is 59 kg, so adjusted body weight = 59 kg. 5. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation (male, no gender factor): CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight (kg)] / (72 × serum creatinine). Age = 53, so (140 – 53) = 87. Numerator = 87 × 59 = 5133. Denominator = 72 × 1.1 = 79.2. CrCl = 5133 / 79.2 ≈ 64.8 mL/min. 6. Round to a practical clinical value: ≈ 65 mL/min.",
  "answer": "65 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Determine BMI: weight 97 kg, height 178 cm → BMI = 97 / (1.78²) ≈ 31 kg/m² (obese). 2. Because BMI > 30, use adjusted body weight (ABW). 3. Calculate ideal body weight (IBW) for a woman: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (height in inches – 60). Height = 5 ft 10 in = 70 in → IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × 10 = 68.5 kg. 4. ABW = IBW + 0.4 × (actual – IBW) = 68.5 kg + 0.4 × (97 kg – 68.5 kg) = 68.5 kg + 0.4 × 28.5 kg = 68.5 kg + 11.4 kg = 79.9 kg. 5. Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight (kg) × 0.85] / (72 × serum creatinine). 6. Plug in values: age = 48, weight = 79.9 kg, serum creatinine = 1.0 mg/dL. Numerator = (140 – 48) × 79.9 × 0.85 = 92 × 79.9 × 0.85 ≈ 6,248.2. Denominator = 72 × 1.0 = 72. 7. CrCl = 6,248.2 / 72 ≈ 86.8 mL/min. 8. Round to a reasonable clinical value: ≈ 87 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈87 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Extract admission data: age = 81 years, sex = female, height = 153 cm (1.53 m), weight = 40 kg, serum creatinine = 1.27 mg/dL. 2. Calculate BMI: BMI = weight / height² = 40 kg / (1.53 m)² ≈ 40 / 2.34 ≈ 17.1 kg/m². This is < 18.5, so the patient is underweight. 3. Because she is underweight, the adjusted body weight (ABW) is set to the actual weight: ABW = 40 kg. 4. Use the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for females: CrCl = [(140 – age) × ABW × 0.85] / (72 × SCr). 5. Compute numerator: (140 – 81) = 59; 59 × 40 kg = 2360; 2360 × 0.85 = 2006. 6. Compute denominator: 72 × 1.27 = 91.44. 7. CrCl = 2006 / 91.44 ≈ 21.9 mL/min. 8. Round to a practical clinical value: about 22 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈22 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Extract admission data: age = 60 years, height = 163.1 cm (1.631 m), weight = 58.5 kg, serum creatinine = 10.7 mg/dL. 2. Calculate BMI = weight / height² = 58.5 kg / (1.631 m)² ≈ 22 kg/m², which is within the normal range (18.5‑24.9). 3. Because BMI is normal, set the adjusted body weight to the lower of ideal body weight (IBW) and actual weight. 4. Compute IBW for a male: IBW = 50 kg + 2.3 kg × (inches over 5 ft). Height in inches = 163.1 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 64.2 in; inches over 5 ft = 64.2 – 60 = 4.2 in. IBW ≈ 50 kg + 2.3 × 4.2 ≈ 59.6 kg. 5. Minimum of IBW (≈59.6 kg) and actual weight (58.5 kg) is 58.5 kg, so adjusted body weight = 58.5 kg. 6. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault formula for males: CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight (kg)] / [72 × serum Cr (mg/dL)]. 7. Plug in the numbers: [(140 – 60) × 58.5] / [72 × 10.7] = (80 × 58.5) / (770.4) = 4680 / 770.4 ≈ 6.07 mL/min. 8. Round to a clinically useful figure: ≈ 6 mL/min.",
  "answer": "≈ 6 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Extract patient data at admission: age = 49 years, sex = female, height = 155.5 cm, actual weight = 61.0 kg, serum creatinine = 1.4 mg/dL. 2. Calculate BMI = weight (kg) / (height (m))^2 = 61 / (1.555)^2 ≈ 25.2 kg/m², which is >24.9 → patient is overweight. 3. Compute ideal body weight (IBW) for women using the metric formula: IBW = 45.5 kg + 0.9 × (height cm – 152) = 45.5 + 0.9 × 3.5 ≈ 48.65 kg. 4. Compute adjusted body weight (ABW) because the patient is overweight: ABW = IBW + 0.4 × (actual weight – IBW) = 48.65 + 0.4 × (61 – 48.65) ≈ 48.65 + 4.94 ≈ 53.6 kg. 5. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for females: CrCl = ((140 – age) × weight × 0.85) / (72 × serum creatinine). Plugging in the numbers: ((140 – 49) × 53.6 × 0.85) / (72 × 1.4) = (91 × 53.6 × 0.85) / 100.8 ≈ 4145 / 100.8 ≈ 41.1 mL/min. 6. Round to a reasonable clinical figure.",
  "answer": "Approximately 41 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Identify the required variables from the admission data: age = 28 years, sex = female, serum creatinine = 1.7 mg/dL, height = 179 cm, weight = 99 kg, BMI = 31 kg/m² (obese). 2. Because BMI > 30, use adjusted body weight (ABW).   a. Convert height to inches: 179 cm ÷ 2.54 = 70.47 in.   b. Calculate ideal body weight (IBW) for women: IBW = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg × (inches – 60) = 45.5 + 2.3 × 10.47 ≈ 69.6 kg.   c. Compute ABW: ABW = IBW + 0.4 × (actual – IBW) = 69.6 + 0.4 × (99 – 69.6) ≈ 81.35 kg. 3. Apply the Cockcroft‑Gault equation for women: CrCl = [(140 – age) × weight × 0.85] ÷ (72 × SCr).   Plug in the numbers: CrCl = [(140 – 28) × 81.35 kg × 0.85] ÷ (72 × 1.7) = [112 × 81.35 × 0.85] ÷ 122.4 ≈ 7744.7 ÷ 122.4 ≈ 63.3 mL/min. 4. Round to a practical clinical value: ≈ 63 mL/min.",
  "answer": "63 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1. Convert the serum creatinine from μmol/L to mg/dL: 134 μmol/L ÷ 88.4 = 1.516 mg/dL. 2. Use the 2021 CKD‑EPI creatinine equation without race: eGFR = 142 × min(Scr/κ,1)^α × max(Scr/κ,1)^‑1.200 × 0.9938^Age. For a male, κ = 0.9 and α = –0.302. 3. Compute Scr/κ = 1.516 ÷ 0.9 = 1.684. Since this is >1, min(Scr/κ,1) = 1 and max(Scr/κ,1) = 1.684. 4. min term: 1^‑0.302 = 1. 5. max term: 1.684^‑1.200 ≈ 0.535. 6. Age factor: 0.9938^70 ≈ 0.647. 7. Multiply all components: eGFR = 142 × 1 × 0.535 × 0.647 ≈ 49 mL/min/1.73 m².",
  "answer": "≈ 49 mL/min/1.73 m²"
}

